\section{BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK}
\label{sec:background}

This section reviews the privacy and efficiency problems that motivate our work.  Standard federated learning exposes model updates across many rounds, creating a growing attack surface.  One-shot federated learning limits communication to a single round, but the transferred knowledge still requires protection.  Differential privacy can protect shared outputs, yet it degrades the distillation signal.  Homomorphic encryption avoids this tradeoff but is too expensive for iterative training.  Our framework combines one-shot communication with CKKS encryption and an HE-compatible student architecture to address all three concerns simultaneously.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Privacy Risks of Multi-Round Federated Learning}
\label{sec:bg_multiround}

Federated learning~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} keeps raw data local but repeatedly transmits model updates between clients and a central server.  Each communication round creates an opportunity for information leakage.

\textbf{Gradient inversion attacks.}  Zhu et al.~\cite{zhu2019deep} show that shared gradients can be inverted to reconstruct training images.  Geiping et al.~\cite{geiping2020inverting} extend this result to trained deep networks, recovering high-resolution images from single gradient vectors using cosine-similarity optimisation.  These attacks succeed even when gradients are averaged over small batches. Zhao et al.~\cite{zhao2020idlg} show that ground-truth labels can be extracted analytically from gradients before any optimisation, removing a key bottleneck in earlier attacks. Recent work by Dimitrov et al.~\cite{dimitrov2024spear} further demonstrates that exact reconstruction is possible for batches of up to $b \lesssim 25$ images by exploiting ReLU-induced gradient sparsity, breaking the assumption that larger batches provide meaningful protection.

\textbf{Inference attacks.}  Nasr et al.~\cite{nasr2019comprehensive} design both passive and active white-box membership inference attacks that exploit intermediate parameter updates during federated training.  In the active setting, a malicious participant can deliberately amplify information leakage across rounds.  Hitaj et al.~\cite{hitaj2017deep} demonstrate a GAN-based attack that progressively reconstructs other participants' training data by exploiting the evolving model dynamics over multiple rounds. Kerkouche et al.~\cite{kerkouche2023property} show that client-specific properties can be inferred from aggregated model updates alone, even without access to individual gradients: a linear model trained across rounds recovers membership information despite secure aggregation being active.

\textbf{Multi-round aggregation leakage.}  Even when secure aggregation protects individual updates, information can accumulate over rounds.  So et al.~\cite{so2023securing} prove that standard random user selection can leak individual user models within $O(N)$ rounds of conventional secure aggregation, and propose Multi-RoundSecAgg to mitigate this. 

These results show that repeated communication is a fundamental source of privacy risk, independent of whether raw data is shared~\cite{carletti2025sok}.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{One-Shot Federated Learning}
\label{sec:bg_oneshot}

One-shot federated learning reduces the communication between clients and server to a single round~\cite{guha2019oneshot, wang2025towards}.  This eliminates the iterative exposure exploited by gradient inversion, GAN reconstruction, and multi-round aggregation attacks.

Knowledge distillation is a natural fit for one-shot settings: clients train local teacher models, then transfer knowledge to a server-side student in one step. Data heterogeneity across clients is a central challenge: when local data distributions differ, the ensemble of teacher logits becomes inconsistent, and naive averaging degrades student accuracy. Several approaches have been proposed. A recent survey of this family of methods is given by Liu et al.~\cite{liu2025oneshot}. FedMD~\cite{li2019fedmd} establishes the public-dataset-mediated distillation paradigm: each client reports soft predictions on a shared unlabelled dataset, and the server trains a central model from these aggregated outputs.
Lin et al.~\cite{lin2020feddf} propose FedDF, which performs ensemble distillation on the server using an unlabelled proxy dataset to fuse heterogeneous client models in a single post-aggregation step.  FedKT~\cite{li2021fedkt} uses a two-tier knowledge transfer framework with differential privacy guarantees.  DENSE~\cite{zhang2022dense} and FedSD2C~\cite{chen2024fedsd2c} generate synthetic data or distillates on the client side.  FedDiff~\cite{feddiff2024} uses diffusion models to navigate heterogeneity in one-shot settings.  FedMD-NFDP~\cite{sun2021fedmdnfdp} achieves noise-free differential privacy through data subsampling.

However, one-shot communication does not automatically protect the transferred knowledge.  If logits, features, or synthetic distillates are sent in plaintext, the server can still inspect them. This applies regardless of whether a public or proxy dataset is used: the client's response on any shared data encodes information about its private training distribution~\cite{shi2025unveiling}.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Server Inference in Federated Distillation}
\label{sec:bg_server_inference}

Even without gradient access, plaintext logits and features leak private information.
This is a central risk for any federated distillation scheme that does not encrypt the knowledge transfer channel. Fredrikson et al.~\cite{fredrikson2015model} establish the root cause: confidence values output by a model carry enough information to invert it, allowing an adversary to recover features of the training data without accessing weights or gradients directly.


Takahashi et al.~\cite{takahashi2023breaching} design a Paired-Logits Inversion attack against FedMD-style distillation.  A malicious server trains an inversion network that exploits the confidence gap between its own predictions and client predictions on a shared public dataset, recovering private facial images with high fidelity.

Shi et al.~\cite{shi2025unveiling} present the first comprehensive privacy analysis of public-dataset-assisted federated distillation.  They introduce label distribution inference attacks and membership inference attacks based on the Likelihood Ratio Attack (LiRA), showing that client logit vectors on the public dataset encode memorisation of private training data.  Their attacks achieve high accuracy against FedMD, DS-FL, and Cronus. Separately, Gu et al.~\cite{gu2023ldia} show that an honest-but-curious server can infer the label distribution of each client from aggregated predictions alone, without reconstructing any individual sample, establishing label distribution as an independent privacy dimension in federated distillation.

Yang et al.~\cite{yang2023fdleaks} propose FD-Leaks, a membership inference attack where a malicious client exploits its local model as a shadow model during federated distillation.  Wang et al.~\cite{wang2024graddiff} further introduce gradient-based membership inference attacks (GradDiff and GradDrift) that can be conducted by both the server and clients. Jagielski et al.~\cite{jagielski2023students} show that knowledge distillation can amplify membership inference vulnerability: distilled student models sometimes exhibit higher attack success rates than their teacher models. Shao et al.~\cite{shao2024selective} further show that switching from soft to hard labels reduces but does not eliminate the risk: clients' discrete predictions on a shared proxy dataset still leak their label distribution to a black-box adversary.



These findings establish that plaintext logit and feature sharing is not privacy-safe, even in one-shot settings where no gradients are exchanged.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Limitations of Differential Privacy for Knowledge Distillation}
\label{sec:bg_dp_limits}

Differential privacy (DP) can protect the outputs shared during distillation by adding calibrated noise.  However, in knowledge-distillation-based federated learning, the soft labels are the sole channel for knowledge transfer.  Corrupting this channel has a direct and measurable cost.

\textbf{Utility degradation.}  Gad et al.~\cite{gad2024communication} systematically evaluate KD-based federated learning under local differential privacy and report up to 70\% accuracy loss on CIFAR-100 at privacy budget $\epsilon = 0.1$.  Wei et al.~\cite{wei2020federated} provide a formal convergence bound showing an inherent, non-vanishing utility gap for any finite privacy budget $\epsilon$.  This gap is particularly problematic in one-shot settings, where the noise cannot be diluted over many communication rounds. Qi et al.~\cite{qi2023privatekt} demonstrate this concretely in the federated distillation setting: their improved PrivateKT method, which uses importance-sampled public data to reduce noise sensitivity, still leaves a residual accuracy gap of up to 16% relative to centralised training at $\epsilon = 2$, confirming that the cost is fundamental rather than purely algorithmic.

\textbf{Disparate impact.}  Bagdasaryan et al.~\cite{bagdasaryan2019differential} show that DP-SGD disproportionately degrades accuracy on underrepresented classes.  In their experiments, an underrepresented class suffers a 20-percentage-point accuracy drop under DP while the majority class is nearly unaffected.  This effect is amplified in federated settings with heterogeneous client data distributions.

\textbf{Analogy from PATE.}  The PATE framework~\cite{papernot2018scalable}, which is structurally analogous to federated distillation (an ensemble of teachers transfers knowledge to a student via noisy aggregation), demonstrates that each noisy label carries a measurable accuracy penalty.  Achieving strong privacy ($\epsilon < 1$) requires noise levels that severely limit distillation quality. Hoech et al.~\cite{hoech2022fedauxfdp} evaluate full differential privacy in a one-shot distillation setting directly, and report that accuracy degrades substantially once $\epsilon \leq 0.1$. Critically, unlike multi-round federated learning, the one-shot setting provides no privacy amplification through iterative composition: the entire privacy budget is consumed in a single exchange.

These results show that the DP noise required to defend against the inference attacks described in Section~\ref{sec:bg_server_inference} would substantially degrade the knowledge distillation signal.  This creates a fundamental privacy--utility dilemma that motivates cryptographic alternatives.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Homomorphic Encryption in Federated Learning}
\label{sec:bg_he_fl}

Homomorphic encryption (HE) protects model updates and intermediate computations cryptographically, without adding noise to the data.  The CKKS scheme~\cite{cheon2017ckks} supports approximate arithmetic over real numbers, making it suitable for machine learning workloads.

\textbf{Iterative HE-FL is expensive.}  Applying HE to standard multi-round federated learning introduces large computational and communication overheads. Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2020batchcrypt} report that naive HE-FL increases training time by two orders of magnitude; their BatchCrypt system reduces this through gradient quantisation and batching, achieving 23$\times$--93$\times$ speedup while still being substantially slower than plaintext training.  Jin et al.~\cite{jin2023fedml} show that selective parameter encryption can reduce overhead by 10$\times$--40$\times$ for large models.  Wei et al.~\cite{wei2025fedshe} address the bandwidth cost of CKKS ciphertext expansion in iterative exchanges.  POSEIDON~\cite{sav2021poseidon} demonstrates full HE-based federated neural network training with multiparty encryption, but the computational cost remains prohibitive for deep models over many rounds. A concurrent line of work combines HE with knowledge distillation rather than gradient aggregation to reduce the volume of encrypted computation: rather than encrypting weight vectors across rounds, clients encrypt only their output predictions for a shared dataset~\cite{huang2025dkdfl}. Even so, this approach still requires multiple encrypted inference passes per round and does not eliminate the communication overhead of ciphertext transmission.

\textbf{CKKS rounding limits depth.}  The CKKS scheme accumulates approximation noise with each multiplicative operation.  This noise is managed through rescaling, which consumes one multiplicative level per operation.  The total multiplicative depth budget constrains the computational complexity that can be evaluated on a single set of ciphertexts.  For deep neural networks, this budget is quickly exhausted, particularly when non-polynomial operations require expensive approximations. The standard cryptographic workaround is bootstrapping~\cite{cheon2018bootstrapping}, which refreshes the multiplicative level budget by re-encrypting the ciphertext. However, bootstrapping itself consumes several levels and introduces additional latency in the order of seconds per call, making it impractical to apply at every layer of a deep network during training.



These constraints make iterative HE-FL impractical for deep models.  However, in a one-shot setting, communication happens once and ciphertexts can be structured around the distillation objective.  Block-independent training with fresh ciphertexts per block avoids level accumulation across the full network.  Recent work by Pirillo and Colombo~\cite{pirillo2025reboot} independently validates this principle for encrypted MLP training, where local-loss blocks with fresh ciphertext sets reduce the required multiplicative depth from $O(H)$ to $O(1)$ per block. Our framework applies the same insight in the federated one-shot distillation setting, where the supervision signal itself is also encrypted.


%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{HE-Compatible Neural Network Design}
\label{sec:bg_he_networks}

A prerequisite for encrypted distillation is a student architecture that can be evaluated under HE constraints.  The core challenge is replacing ReLU and batch normalisation, which are not polynomial, with operations that require only addition and multiplication. CryptoNets~\cite{gilad2016cryptonets} establish the foundational protocol: a neural network trained with polynomial activations is applied directly to CKKS-encrypted inputs, returning an encrypted prediction. All subsequent HE-compatible architectures refine this template.

GAZELLE~\cite{juvekar2018gazelle} addresses this by using HE for linear layers and garbled circuits for nonlinear activations, achieving three orders of magnitude speedup over CryptoNets.  However, this hybrid approach requires interactive multi-round protocols.

Obla et al.~\cite{obla2020effective} systematically construct polynomial activation functions for HE-friendly CNNs and demonstrate their effectiveness on CIFAR-10 under CKKS.  Garimella et al.~\cite{garimella2021sisyphus} identify the escaping activation problem where polynomial activations cause exponentially growing magnitudes in deeper networks, showing that naive ReLU replacement fails for architectures beyond a few layers.  Baruch et al.~\cite{baruch2022methodology} combine learnable polynomial activations with knowledge distillation from a pre-trained ReLU teacher, recovering accuracy lost from polynomial substitution on AlexNet and SqueezeNet.  Lee et al.~\cite{lee2022privacy} demonstrate privacy-preserving inference with polynomial activations on ResNet-20 using fully homomorphic encryption. The batch normalisation layer poses a separate challenge: it is not polynomial, but it can be made HE-compatible by absorbing its parameters into the preceding linear layer at inference time~\cite{ibarrondo2021fhebn}.  This layer-folding technique eliminates the BN computation from the encrypted circuit entirely and is a standard preprocessing step for HE-compatible deployment of ResNet-style architectures. More recently, Al Hossain and Rahman~\cite{alhossain2025stablepolynomial} propose a training framework that combines a Boundary Loss with Selective Gradient Clipping to stabilise polynomial activations of degree up to 22 in deep ResNet variants, achieving accuracy within 1% of ReLU baselines and confirming HE-compatibility of the trained models.

These works establish the building blocks for HE-compatible architectures but focus exclusively on centralised inference or training. None addresses the problem of training such architectures in a federated distillation setting where the supervision signal itself arrives as a ciphertext. Our method addresses this gap.



