\section{BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK}
\label{sec:background}

This section reviews the privacy and efficiency challenges motivating this work. Standard federated learning exposes model updates across multiple rounds, expanding the attack surface. One-shot federated learning restricts communication to a single round, yet the transferred knowledge still requires protection. While differential privacy can safeguard shared outputs, it degrades the distillation signal. Homomorphic encryption avoids this tradeoff but remains computationally prohibitive for iterative training. To address these issues simultaneously, our framework combines one-shot communication, CKKS encryption, and an HE-compatible student architecture.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Privacy Risks of Multi-Round Federated Learning}
\label{sec:bg_multiround}

Federated learning~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} retains raw data locally but repeatedly transmits model updates, with each round presenting an opportunity for information leakage.

\textbf{Gradient inversion attacks.} Shared gradients can be inverted to reconstruct training images~\cite{zhu2019deep}. This vulnerability extends to trained deep networks, allowing high-resolution image recovery from single gradient vectors via cosine-similarity optimisation~\cite{geiping2020inverting}. Such attacks remain effective even when gradients are averaged over small batches. Furthermore, ground-truth labels can be analytically extracted from gradients prior to optimisation~\cite{zhao2020idlg}. Recent findings also demonstrate exact image reconstruction for batches up to $b \lesssim 25$ by exploiting ReLU-induced gradient sparsity~\cite{dimitrov2024spear}, invalidating the assumption that larger batches assure protection.

\textbf{Inference attacks.} Active and passive white-box membership inference attacks can exploit intermediate parameter updates during training~\cite{nasr2019comprehensive}. Malicious participants can deliberately amplify this leakage. GAN-based attacks can progressively reconstruct training data by exploiting evolving model dynamics across rounds~\cite{hitaj2017deep}. Additionally, client-specific properties can be inferred solely from aggregated updates; a linear model can recover membership information even when secure aggregation is active~\cite{kerkouche2023property}.

\textbf{Multi-round aggregation leakage.} Even with secure aggregation, information accumulates over time. Standard random user selection can leak individual user models within $O(N)$ rounds, prompting mitigations like Multi-RoundSecAgg~\cite{so2023securing}. 

Consequently, repeated communication is inherently risky, regardless of raw data sharing policies~\cite{carletti2025sok}.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{One-Shot Federated Learning}
\label{sec:bg_oneshot}

One-shot federated learning limits client-server communication to a single round~\cite{guha2019oneshot, wang2025towards}, eliminating the iterative exposure that facilitates multi-round attacks.

Knowledge distillation suits one-shot settings: clients train local teachers and transfer knowledge to a server-side student in a single step. However, data heterogeneity causes inconsistent teacher logits, degrading student accuracy upon naive averaging. Various methods address this~\cite{liu2025oneshot}. Public-dataset approaches such as FedMD~\cite{li2019fedmd} and FedDF~\cite{lin2020feddf} have clients provide soft predictions on a shared unlabelled set for server-side ensemble distillation. Synthetic-data methods generate training samples on the client side, including DENSE~\cite{zhang2022dense}, FedSD2C~\cite{chen2024fedsd2c}, and diffusion-based FedDiff~\cite{feddiff2024} and FedDrip~\cite{huangsuwan2025feddrip}. Privacy-augmented variants add differential privacy to the distillation channel, as in FedKT~\cite{li2021fedkt} and FedMD-NFDP~\cite{sun2021fedmdnfdp}.

Diffusion-based approaches introduce their own concerns. Training or running diffusion models locally imposes substantial computational overhead on clients, limiting practical deployment. More critically, diffusion models are known to memorise and regurgitate training examples~\cite{carlini2023extracting}, so the synthetic data they produce may inadvertently leak private information. Soft-label distillation channels can similarly transfer memorised data through temperature-dependent information~\cite{behrens2025memorized}. These observations indicate that neither synthetic data nor soft labels are inherently safe knowledge carriers.

Despite these advances, one-shot communication does not inherently secure the transferred knowledge. Sending plaintext logits, features, or distillates allows server inspection. A client's response on any shared dataset uniquely encodes its private training distribution~\cite{shi2025unveiling}.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Server Inference in Federated Distillation}
\label{sec:bg_server_inference}

Plaintext logits and features leak private information even without gradient access, posing a central risk to unencrypted federated distillation. Model confidence values inherently provide sufficient information for inversion attacks, enabling adversaries to reconstruct training data features directly~\cite{fredrikson2015model}.

Against FedMD-style distillation, the Paired-Logits Inversion attack~\cite{takahashi2023breaching} allows a malicious server to recover high-fidelity private images by exploiting the confidence gap between its predictions and those of the clients. Shi et al.~\cite{shi2025unveiling} demonstrate that client logit vectors encode the memorisation of private data, enabling highly accurate label distribution and membership inference attacks against FedMD, DS-FL, and Cronus. Additionally, an honest-but-curious server can infer a client's label distribution solely from aggregated predictions without reconstructing individual samples~\cite{gu2023ldia}.

Membership inference attacks further exacerbate these risks. FD-Leaks~\cite{yang2023fdleaks} uses a malicious client's local model as a shadow model, while GradDiff and GradDrift~\cite{wang2024graddiff} enable gradient-based inference by servers and clients. Distillation amplifies these vulnerabilities: students can exhibit up to $+9.81\%$ higher MIA susceptibility than their teachers~\cite{cui2025mia}, and the GLiRA framework~\cite{galichin2025glira} demonstrates that knowledge-distillation-based shadow models improve likelihood-ratio attacks over the original LiRA baseline~\cite{carlini2022membership}. Co-operative LiRA and distillation-based LiRA variants~\cite{shi2025unveiling} achieve high true-positive rates at low false-positive rates against FedMD- and DS-FL-style protocols. Even transmitting hard labels instead of soft labels only mitigates, rather than eliminates, this risk; discrete predictions still leak label distributions~\cite{shao2024selective}.

\textbf{Beyond privacy: poisoning and model stealing.} The attack surface extends beyond privacy violations. Logit poisoning attacks~\cite{tang2024logits} allow adversaries to manipulate shared predictions and corrupt the distilled student model. A semi-honest server can reconstruct client models from observed logits~\cite{wan2024privacy}, enabling model stealing. Even parametric feature sharing is unsafe: feature inversion can recover private images with SSIM values approaching $0.997$~\cite{beitollahi2024parametric}. On the defensive side, vanilla federated distillation can tolerate up to $45\%$ Byzantine clients due to the bounded simplex property of soft labels~\cite{roux2025byzantine}; however, targeted HIPS attacks circumvent existing Byzantine defenses.

This diverse attack landscape demonstrates that plaintext knowledge transfer---whether logits, soft labels, features, or synthetic data---is fundamentally unsafe regardless of format. Local differential privacy can technically bound leakage, but at meaningful privacy levels ($\epsilon \leq 1$) it reduces FedMD to random-guessing accuracy~\cite{sun2021fedmdnfdp}. Only cryptographic protection eliminates these attack vectors entirely without degrading the distillation signal.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Limitations of Differential Privacy for Knowledge Distillation}
\label{sec:bg_dp_limits}

While differential privacy (DP) mitigates leakage by adding calibrated noise, soft labels serve as the sole knowledge transfer channel in federated distillation. Corrupting these labels imposes a direct utility cost.

\textbf{Utility degradation.} Applying local DP to knowledge distillation causes severe accuracy drops (e.g., up to 70\% on CIFAR-100 at $\epsilon = 0.1$~\cite{gad2024communication}). Formal bounds confirm a non-vanishing utility gap for any finite $\epsilon$~\cite{wei2020federated}. This gap is acute in one-shot settings since noise cannot be diluted across rounds. Even optimised approaches like PrivateKT~\cite{qi2023privatekt} leave a residual 16\% accuracy gap at $\epsilon = 2$, indicating a fundamental limitation.

\textbf{Disparate impact.} DP disproportionately degrades accuracy for underrepresented classes. For instance, DP-SGD can decrease minor class accuracy by 20 percentage points while leaving majority classes unaffected~\cite{bagdasaryan2019differential}. This disparity is exacerbated by data heterogeneity in federated settings.

\textbf{Analogy from PATE.} In the PATE framework~\cite{papernot2018scalable}---which resembles federated distillation---strong privacy ($\epsilon < 1$) requires noise levels that severely diminish distillation quality. Direct evaluations of one-shot DP distillation confirm substantial accuracy losses when $\epsilon \leq 0.1$~\cite{hoech2022fedauxfdp}. Crucially, one-shot settings consume the entire privacy budget immediately, precluding the privacy amplification observed in iterative contexts.

Because the noise required to thwart inference attacks (Section~\ref{sec:bg_server_inference}) severely degrades the distillation signal, a critical privacy--utility dilemma emerges, motivating cryptographic solutions.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Homomorphic Encryption in Federated Learning}
\label{sec:bg_he_fl}

Homomorphic encryption (HE) cryptographically secures computations without injecting noise. The CKKS scheme~\cite{cheon2017ckks} is uniquely suited for machine learning due to its support for approximate real-number arithmetic.

\textbf{Iterative HE-FL is expensive.} Applying HE to multi-round federated learning incurs severe computational and communication overheads. While systems like BatchCrypt~\cite{zhang2020batchcrypt} accelerate training via quantisation and batching, they remain significantly slower than plaintext methods. Selectively encrypting parameters~\cite{jin2023fedml} and optimising communication protocols~\cite{wei2025fedshe} reduce overhead, but training deep networks over many rounds remains prohibitive~\cite{sav2021poseidon}. Alternatively, combining HE with knowledge distillation rather than gradient aggregation limits encrypted computation volumes~\cite{huang2025dkdfl}. However, this still requires multiple encrypted inference passes per round and retains the ciphertext communication burden.

\textbf{Intermediate result exposure.} Even when individual updates are encrypted, multi-round HE-FL reveals aggregate model states between rounds. Participating clients can analyse the sequence of aggregates they receive to infer other clients' contributions, creating an attack vector that grows with the number of communication rounds. One-shot protocols eliminate this vector entirely by limiting communication to a single encrypted upload.

\textbf{CKKS rounding limits depth.} CKKS accumulates approximation noise during multiplications, necessitating rescaling that consumes a finite multiplicative depth budget. For deep networks, non-polynomial approximations rapidly exhaust this budget. While bootstrapping~\cite{cheon2018bootstrapping} refreshes this budget, it introduces substantial latency per call, rendering layer-wise application impractical during training.

Although iterative HE-FL is inefficient for deep models, one-shot settings allow ciphertexts to uniquely target the distillation objective. Block-independent training with fresh ciphertexts circumvents deep level accumulation. This principle was recently validated for encrypted MLP training~\cite{pirillo2025reboot}, where local-loss blocks reduce the multiplicative depth from $O(H)$ to $O(1)$. Our framework applies this insight to federated one-shot distillation: progressive stacking trains each block independently with fresh ciphertexts, confining the depth budget to a single block rather than the full network.

%% ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{HE-Compatible Neural Network Design}
\label{sec:bg_he_networks}

Encrypted distillation requires an HE-compatible student architecture. The primary challenge is replacing non-polynomial operations, such as ReLU and batch normalisation (BN), with addition and multiplication. Originating with CryptoNets~\cite{gilad2016cryptonets}, foundational protocols apply networks trained with polynomial activations to CKKS-encrypted inputs.

To improve efficiency, GAZELLE~\cite{juvekar2018gazelle} combines HE for linear layers with garbled circuits for non-linearities, though this requires interactive multi-round protocols. Purely HE-friendly CNNs systematically utilise polynomial activations~\cite{obla2020effective}. However, applying naive polynomial substitutions to deep networks causes exponentially growing magnitudes, termed the escaping activation problem~\cite{garimella2021sisyphus}. Mitigations include learnable polynomial activations paired with distillation from ReLU teachers~\cite{baruch2022methodology}, and stable training frameworks employing boundary losses and selective gradient clipping for deep ResNets~\cite{alhossain2025stablepolynomial}. For privacy-preserving inference, polynomial activations have proven functional on models such as ResNet-20~\cite{lee2022privacy}. Separately, BN layers are made HE-compatible via layer-folding, where BN parameters are absorbed into preceding linear layers at inference time, removing the operation from the encrypted circuit~\cite{ibarrondo2021fhebn}.

An additional challenge arises in multi-client federated settings: different data parties produce features with incompatible magnitude distributions, making standard batch normalisation layer-folding inapplicable since it assumes access to centralised statistics. Normalisation strategies must instead operate either locally per client or collaboratively across clients without revealing private statistics.

While these studies provide components for HE-compatible architectures, they focus solely on centralised inference or training. Existing literature does not address training these architectures in a federated distillation setting where the supervision signal is an encrypted ciphertext. Our work bridges this gap.
